      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A human rights framework can provide a moral basis for intervention. International human rights law, as established by the United Nations and various treaties, does imply a moral obligation to protect vulnerable populations from human rights abuses. In cases where an autocrat's rise to power would involve widespread human rights abuses, this framework can justify intervention to prevent harm and protect global democracy. This perspective argues that NATO's \"responsibility to protect\" doctrine, established in 2005, provides a moral justification for preemptive intervention in such situations.",
      "source": "target",
